        "knowledge_point_id": "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis",
        "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=4{x}^{3}-9x-1$ at the point $(1,-6)$ intersects the $x$-axis.",
        "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-9x-1.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{3}-9x-1)=12{x}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=12(1)^{2}-9=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =3(x-1) \\\\ y & =3x-3-6 \\\\ y & =3x-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =3x-9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $(3,0).$",
